<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franz Joseph I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Franz Joseph · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">House of Habsburg-Lorraine</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franz Joseph I was Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> (r. 1848–1916). He came to the throne during the revolutions of 1848, agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> that created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Dual Monarchy</a>, and led the Austro-Hungarian Empire into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. His long reign symbolised the continuity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i.jpg" alt="Franz Joseph I" width="300" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Pietzner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Joseph_I_of_Austria.1910_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Franz Joseph was born on 18 August 1830 at Schönbrunn Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the eldest son of Archduke Franz Karl and Sophie, Princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. His uncle Ferdinand, emperor from 1835, was disabled by seizures and his father was unambitious, so his politically ambitious mother raised him as a future emperor, stressing devotion, responsibility and diligence. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a> he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-radetzky-von-radetz">Field Marshal Radetzky</a>'s campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and received his baptism of fire at Santa Lucia on 5 May. In December 1848 Ferdinand abdicated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-zu-schwarzenberg">Felix zu Schwarzenberg</a>'s plan to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1848">Hungarian Revolution of 1848</a>, and Franz Joseph took the throne at eighteen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Largely considered a reactionary, he spent his early reign resisting constitutionalism. After Schwarzenberg's death in 1852 the emperor effectively became his own prime minister, and he was forced to issue the February Patent of 1861 to borrow money abroad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> lost influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italian-war-of-independence">Second Italian War of Independence</a> in 1859 and the war of 1866. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866 ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1866">Peace of Prague</a>, which settled the German Question in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, though he ceded no territory to it. His main foreign-policy goal had been unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. In 1854 he married his cousin Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, whom he had chosen in place of the bride his mother favoured.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> he granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> greater autonomy and created the dual monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, which he then ruled peacefully for 45 years. In 1873 he joined the League of Three Emperors with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II of Russia</a>. His troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878, and in 1908 he annexed it, causing the Bosnian Crisis. In 1903 he used the last veto by a Catholic ruler on a papal election, against Cardinal Rampolla.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He suffered personal tragedies: the execution of his brother Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1867, the suicide of his son Rudolf in 1889, and the assassinations of his wife Elisabeth in 1898 and of his nephew and heir, Archduke Franz Ferdinand, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> on 28 June 1914. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>'s declaration of war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> then began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Told of the killing, he said that a superior power had restored the order he had been unable to maintain.</p><p>Franz Joseph died at Schönbrunn on the evening of 21 November 1916, at the age of 86, of pneumonia caught after a walk with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-iii-of-bavaria">Ludwig III of Bavaria</a>. After almost 68 years on the throne he was succeeded by his grandnephew Charles I, and he is buried in the Imperial Crypt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Franz Josef Land in the Arctic was named for him in 1873.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franz Joseph I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna, with several hundred thousand inhabitants, was the political and cultural centre, and in 1857 <b>Franz Joseph</b> ordered its old fortifications demolished to make way for the Ringstrasse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ausgleich, or Compromise, of 1867 was negotiated between Emperor <b>Franz Joseph I</b> and Hungarian liberal leaders, above all Ferenc Deák and Count Gyula Andrássy, who recognised that a weakened Austrian Empire needed Hungarian cooperation and that Hungary could extract far-reaching autonomy from a dynasty in crisis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Habsburg lands Metternich resigned and went into exile, Ferdinand I abdicated in December 1848 in favour of <b>Franz Joseph I</b>, and Lajos Kossuth led the Hungarian government.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1849 the Slovak National Council petitioned Emperor <b>Franz Joseph I</b> to recognise the Slovak nation within defined borders, and he replied only with a general reference to the equality of all his peoples.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-slavism">Pan-Slavism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Austria's defeat by Prussia in 1866 forced <b>Franz Joseph</b> to settle with the Hungarian leaders Ferenc Deák and Gyula Andrássy.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1867-1918">Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918)</a></li><li><blockquote>After French troops took Mexico City in 1863, Napoleon III arranged for an Assembly of Notables to offer the crown to Archduke Maximilian of Austria, brother of <b>Franz Joseph</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-mexican-empire">Second Mexican Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Schönbrunn Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Schönbrunn Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.19,16.31&amp;year=1882" aria-label="Schönbrunn Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4929 478 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Franz Joseph I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5379 748h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5379 748h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.19,16.31&amp;year=1882">What was here in 1882? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
